    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.k_core - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\":\n    \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.k_core.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.k_core()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Extract the k-core subgraph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.k_core()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Extract the k-core subgraph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.k_core()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Extract the k-core subgraph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nExtract the k-core subgraph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L57032-L57130){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.k_core(k)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![k-core](../assets/graphs/k-core.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: an eight-node graph with a dense four-node cluster A, B, C, D, a ring E, F, G and a pendant H. Right: the 3-core, the part in which every node keeps at least three neighbors, is the cluster A, B, C, D in the accent color; E to H and their edges are peeled away and drawn dashed.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe k-core is the maximal subgraph where all nodes have degree >= k. Iteratively removes\nnodes with degree < k until all remaining nodes satisfy the condition.\n\nRemoving a node lowers the degree of its neighbors, which can push them below `k` in\nturn, so peeling continues until nothing more changes. What remains is the densely\ninterconnected heart of the network, stripped of its periphery. The k-cores are nested:\nthe `(k+1)`-core is always contained in the k-core.\n\nThe k-core is useful for:\n\n- finding the cohesive core of a social, collaboration, or protein interaction network\n- removing low-degree noise (pendant nodes, chains, sparse fringes) before analysis or\n  visualization\n- identifying influential spreaders, which tend to sit in the innermost cores\n\nTo get the core number of every node in one pass instead of extracting one core at a\ntime, use `coreness()`; related extractions are `k_shell()` (coreness exactly `k`),\n`k_crust()` (coreness at most `k`), and `k_truss()` (an edge-based analogue).\n\nDegrees count edges without weights. For directed graphs, degree means out-degree. The\noriginal graph is not modified. The returned graph keeps the node and edge IDs, labels,\nweights and attributes of the original.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Minimum degree required for nodes to remain in the k-core. Must be a non-negative\n    integer. `k=0` keeps the whole graph, `k=1` drops isolated nodes, and `k=2` removes\n    all trees hanging off the graph (pendant nodes and chains), leaving only nodes on or\n    between cycles. A `k` larger than the graph's maximum coreness returns an empty graph.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   New graph containing only the k-core nodes and the edges between them.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~coreness`) \n\n:   The largest `k` for which each node is in the `k`-core.\n\n[](`~k_shell`) \n\n:   The nodes of the `k`-core that aren't in the next one.\n\n[](`~k_crust`) \n\n:   The nodes left once the `k+1`-core is removed.\n\n[](`~k_truss`) \n\n:   A stricter core, where each edge must lie in triangles.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThis graph has a dense cluster `A`-`D`, a loop through `E`, `F` and `G`, and a pendant\nnode `H`. The 2-core peels away `H`. For the 3-core, `F` and `G` fall below three\nneighbors, which leaves `E` with only `C` and `D`, so it goes too:\n\n::: {#734bc1a4 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"D\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"D\"),\n     (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"C\", \"E\"), (\"D\", \"E\"), (\"E\", \"F\"), (\"F\", \"G\"),\n     (\"G\", \"D\"), (\"G\", \"H\")]\n)\n\n{\n    k: sorted(graph.k_core(k).get_node_df()[\"label\"].to_list())\n    for k in [2, 3]\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{2: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E', 'F', 'G'], 3: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D']}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA path has no cycle, so peeling its ends eventually removes everything:\n\n::: {#d6f105c5 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nec.Graph().add_path_graph(6).k_core(2).count_nodes()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n0\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### The barricade\n\n`les_miserables()` links two characters of Victor Hugo's novel when they appear in the\nsame chapter. Its 9-core is the group that keeps nine links each among themselves, the\ntwelve characters at the barricade. They are nearly all linked to one another, with 62\nof the 66 possible links:\n\n::: {#9b2580a0 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nnovel = ec.les_miserables()\nbarricade = novel.k_core(9)\n\nbarricade.count_nodes(), barricade.count_edges(), barricade.density()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(12, 62, 0.9393939393939394)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#8519c581 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nbarricade.plot(label_by=\"label\", seed=1)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmln